:-x Palindrome checker in Smile
:-x
:-x This is the example where a deque genuinely beats a plain stack:
:-x we repeatedly compare the LEFT-most and the RIGHT-most character
:-x and throw both away, working our way inward from both ends.
:-x A stack can only reach one end, so it cannot do this directly.
:-x
:-x Trick: `o-8` (rotate_left) moves the left-most value to the right
:-x end, so the two right-most values become (old-right, old-left).
:-x Then `:=)` (equal_right) compares exactly that outer pair.

:-x ---- push the word "ABBA" onto the deque, left to right ----
:-p 6-) 5-)   :-x A (65)
:-p 6-) 6-)   :-x B (66)
:-p 6-) 6-)   :-x B (66)
:-p 6-) 5-)   :-x A (65)

:-x ---- fold inward: compare the outer pair, discard both, repeat ----
:-x The loop peeks the left end; letters are nonzero, so it runs
:-x until the deque is empty (works for even-length words).
[-:
  o-8                 :-x rotate left-most char to the right end
  :=)                 :-x compare the two right-most (orig right vs orig left)
  :-{                 :-x pop that flag from the right:
    :-x match -> do nothing, keep folding inward
  :-|
    :-x mismatch -> print "No\n" and stop
    :-p 7-) 8-) :-o   :-x N
    :-p 1-) 1-) 1-) :-o   :-x o
    :-p 1-) 0-) :-o   :-x newline
    B-)
  }-:
:-]

:-x ---- the deque drained with no mismatch: it is a palindrome ----
:-p 8-) 9-) :-o       :-x Y
:-p 1-) 0-) 1-) :-o   :-x e
:-p 1-) 1-) 5-) :-o   :-x s
:-p 1-) 0-) :-o       :-x newline
